## Proof

Let $n = 2012$. Denote the entry in row $i$, column $j$ by $a_{ij} \in [0,1]$. Let $S$ be the total sum, $c_j = \sum_i a_{ij}$ the column sums, and $r_i = \sum_j a_{ij}$ the row sums.

**Column condition.** A vertical cut after column $k$ ($1 \le k \le n-1$) produces a left piece with sum $P_k = c_1 + \cdots + c_k$ and a right piece with sum $S - P_k$. The requirement is: for every $k$, $\min(P_k,\, S-P_k) \le 1$, i.e., $P_k \le 1$ or $P_k \ge S-1$. Since $P_k$ is nondecreasing in $k$, every prefix sum lies in $[0,1] \cup [S-1, S]$.

**Case $S \le 2$:** then $[0,1]\cup[S-1,S] = [0,S]$, so the column (and row) condition is automatic. Since we will achieve $S=5 > 2$, this case is suboptimal.

**Case $S > 2$:** there is a gap $(1,\, S-1)$ that no prefix can enter. Since $P_0 = 0 \le 1$ and $P_n = S$, by monotonicity there exists an index $m_c$ such that $P_k \le 1$ for $k \le m_c$ and $P_k \ge S-1$ for $k \ge m_c+1$. Setting $C = m_c+1$ (the **heavy column**):
$$\sum_{j < C} c_j \le 1, \qquad \sum_{j > C} c_j = S - P_C \le 1, \qquad c_C \ge S - 2.$$
Likewise, the row condition yields a **heavy row** $R$ with
$$\sum_{i < R} r_i \le 1, \qquad \sum_{i > R} r_i \le 1, \qquad r_R \ge S-2.$$

**Upper bound.** Partition the grid using row $R$ and column $C$:

| Region | Definition | Sum |
|--------|-----------|-----|
| NW | rows $<R$, cols $<C$ | $x$ |
| NE | rows $<R$, cols $>C$ | $y$ |
| SW | rows $>R$, cols $<C$ | $z$ |
| SE | rows $>R$, cols $>C$ | $w$ |
| cross segments | $u_1$ (row $R$, cols $<C$), $u_2$ (row $R$, cols $>C$), $v_1$ (rows $<R$, col $C$), $v_2$ (rows $>R$, col $C$) | $u_1{+}u_2{+}v_1{+}v_2$ |
| intersection | $a_{RC}$ | $p$ |

The four boundary constraints become:
$$x + y + v_1 \le 1 \quad(\text{rows } <R),\qquad z + w + v_2 \le 1 \quad(\text{rows } >R),$$
$$x + z + u_1 \le 1 \quad(\text{cols } <C),\qquad y + w + u_2 \le 1 \quad(\text{cols } >C).$$

Adding all four: $2(x+y+z+w) + (u_1+u_2+v_1+v_2) \le 4$. Let $A = x+y+z+w$ and $B = u_1+u_2+v_1+v_2$. Then $2A + B \le 4$, and
$$S = A + B + p \le A + (4 - 2A) + 1 = 5 - A \le 5,$$
since $A \ge 0$ and $p \le 1$ (single cell). Hence $S \le 5$.

**Construction achieving $S = 5$.** Take $R = C = 2$ (any interior choice works since $n \ge 3$). Set:
$$a_{22} = 1,\quad a_{21} = 1,\quad a_{23} = 1,\quad a_{12} = 1,\quad a_{32} = 1,$$
and all other entries $0$. The grid (showing the relevant $3\times 3$ block) is
$$\begin{pmatrix} 0&1&0\\ 1&1&1\\ 0&1&0 \end{pmatrix}$$
embedded in the $2012\times 2012$ grid. Column sums: $c_1=1,\ c_2=3,\ c_3=1$, all others $0$. Row sums: $r_1=1,\ r_2=3,\ r_3=1$, all others $0$.

*Vertical cuts:* for $k < 2$, left sum $\le c_1 = 1 \le 1$. For $k = 2$, right sum $= 1 \le 1$. For $k > 2$, right sum $\le c_3 = 1 \le 1$.  
*Horizontal cuts:* symmetric.  
All entries lie in $[0,1]$, and $S = 5$.

Therefore the maximum possible value is $\boxed{5}$.

